# Architecture design principles

Applies when editing **design docs** and **class architecture** in this repo. Implementation C# follows the shared Unity rules (`unity-clean-code-principles.mdc`, `unity-csharp-language.mdc`, hard-linked from `griddungeon-game`).

Complements [game phase](../../docs/02-systems/game-phase.md), [class design](../../docs/05-class-design.md), [ADR 017](../../decisions/017-game-phase-controller.md).

## Single responsibility (SRP)

| Concern | One owner — do not merge |
|---------|---------------------------|
| Macro phase (Hub / Exploration / Combat) | `GamePhaseController` + one `IPhaseController` per phase |
| Combat round (Protocol on core turn → AGI → end-of-round) | `CombatController` only |
| Grid step / facing | `DungeonExplorer` |
| Map reveal state | `MapSystem` + `MapRevealCalculator` (Core) |
| FOE patrol / contact | `FoeSystem` |
| FOE flee retreat cell (walkable?) | `RetreatCellCalculator` (Core); `FoeSystem.CanRetreatFromFoe` delegates |
| FOE flee post-combat placement (FOE contact only) | `FoeFleeRetreatPlacement` (Core); `ExplorationPhaseController` applies on flee end |
| Damage / hit / status math | Core simulators (`DamageCalculator`, `StatusSystem`, …) |
| S1 stratum spawn / story gates / VN effect execution | `GridDungeon.Campaign` (`S1CampaignResolver`, `StoryEventEffectExecutor`, …) — not Core |
| Scene composition / lifetime refs | `GameState` (composition root, not phase logic) |
| Battle arena visuals | `CombatScenePresenter` (not `CombatController`) |
| Input action maps per mode | `InputRouter` |

Reject designs that put combat formulas, map reveal, or phase transitions in one “manager” type.

## DRY

- **One formula** for damage, AGI order, retreat cell, map reveal — document once in Core; Runtime calls simulators.
- **One content ID table** — [content IDs](../../docs/03-content/content-ids.md#content-ids-locked); do not duplicate IDs across docs without linking.
- **One phase flow doc** — [game-phase.md](../../docs/02-systems/game-phase.md) for diagrams; ADR 017 for the decision; class design for type sketches.
- Tolerate a single duplicated diagram if it aids readability; do not fork transition rules in three places.

## KISS / YAGNI

- Current macro flow: **pure C#** `GamePhaseController` — no UVS state graph as authority ([ADR 017](../../decisions/017-game-phase-controller.md)).
- No generic “state machine framework” for three phases.
- Defer optional systems (UVS presentation layer, guest combat, minigames) until spec checklist says otherwise.
- `GridDungeon.Core` stays free of `UnityEngine` and ScriptableObject types — use DTOs at simulator boundaries.

## Fix causes, not symptoms

Architecture docs should name **who initiates transitions** and **who owns state**, not only UI symptoms (e.g. “hide dungeon view” → `CombatPhaseController.OnEnter` + `DungeonView.SetVisible`).

## Be consistent

- Match locked ADRs and [release scope](../../docs/00-release-scope.md); if class design contradicts an ADR, fix class design.
- Use the same type names across `game-phase.md`, `05-class-design.md`, and `04-tech-notes.md` (`CombatScenePresenter`, not `CombatSceneController`).

## When proposing new types

1. Which **game phase** is active?
2. Does it belong in **Core** (generic simulators + DTOs), **Campaign** (stratum/story behavior), or **Runtime** (Unity)?
3. Is there already a subsystem in the module tree ([04-tech-notes](../../docs/04-tech-notes.md))?

Campaign/story placement: see `unity-core-campaign-assembly.mdc`.

If none apply, question whether the type is required.
